.TH exfatcombine 1
.SH NAME
exfatcombine \- append one file to another on exFAT without copying data
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B exfatcombine
[
.B \-v
] [
.B \-V
] [
.B \-h
]
.I DST
.I SRC
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B exfatcombine
moves the clusters of
.I SRC
to the end of
.I DST
on an exFAT filesystem using the
.B EXFAT_IOC_COMBINE
ioctl. Only the FAT and the directory entries are updated; the file data
is not copied, so the time it takes does not depend on the size of
.IR SRC .
On success,
.I DST
grows by the size of
.I SRC
and
.I SRC
is removed: the kernel leaves it as an empty file and
.B exfatcombine
then removes it.
.PP
exFAT can only append clusters at a cluster boundary. If the size of
.I DST
is not a multiple of the cluster size, the kernel first pads
.I DST
with zeroes up to the next cluster boundary, so the combined file contains
a zero-filled gap between the original data of
.I DST
and the data of
.IR SRC .
Applications have to account for that gap, for example by padding
.I DST
themselves with data that their file format skips. The cluster size is
reported as the I/O block size by
.BR stat (1).
.PP
Both files must be regular files on the same exFAT filesystem.
.I SRC
is opened for reading and writing,
.I DST
for writing.
Removing
.I SRC
needs the same permissions as
.BR rm (1).
If it fails, the data has already been appended to
.IR DST ,
.I SRC
is left as an empty file and
.B exfatcombine
exits with an error.
.PP
After a crash during the operation, both files may refer to the same
clusters. Run
.BR fsck.exfat (8)
before modifying or removing
.IR SRC .
It keeps the data in one of the two files. After a crash right after the
operation,
.I SRC
may be left as an empty file; it can then be removed.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print the offset in
.I DST
at which the data of
.I SRC
now starts, and the size of the zero padding.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version number and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B exfatcombine
exits with 0 on success and 1 on failure. It fails with "not supported" if
the running kernel does not provide
.BR EXFAT_IOC_COMBINE .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fsck.exfat (8),
.BR ioctl (2),
.BR stat (1)
